 {
  "key": "complex.de-moivre.q15",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amaths.complex.de-moivre"
  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 5,
  "estimatedSeconds": 450,
  "stem": "Prove by mathematical induction that $(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^n = \\cos n\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin n\\theta$ for every positive integer $n$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "B1 basis: $n = 1$ gives $\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta$ on both sides.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 assumes the result for $n = k$ and writes $(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^{k+1} = (\\cos k\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin k\\theta)(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "M1 expands and collects real and imaginary parts, using $\\mathrm{i}^2 = -1$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p4",
      "description": "A1 uses the compound-angle formulae to reach $\\cos(k + 1)\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin(k + 1)\\theta$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p5",
      "description": "A1 concludes: true for n = 1, and true for k implies true for k + 1, so true for all positive integers n.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "**Basis.** For $n = 1$: LHS $= \\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta =$ RHS.\n\n**Assumption.** Suppose $(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^k = \\cos k\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin k\\theta$ for some positive integer $k$.\n\n**Step.**\n$$\n(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^{k+1} = (\\cos k\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin k\\theta)(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n$$\n= (\\cos k\\theta\\cos\\theta - \\sin k\\theta\\sin\\theta) + \\mathrm{i}(\\sin k\\theta\\cos\\theta + \\cos k\\theta\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n$$\n= \\cos(k + 1)\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin(k + 1)\\theta\n$$\n\n**Conclusion.** The result is true for $n = 1$, and if it is true for $n = k$ it is true for $n = k + 1$. Hence it is true for every positive integer $n$."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The compound-angle formulae do the work in the inductive step.",
  "hints": [
   "Multiply the n = k result by one more factor (cos θ + i sin θ)."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "calculator": false
 },
 {
  "key": "complex.de-moivre.q16",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amaths.complex.de-moivre"
  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 3,
  "estimatedSeconds": 270,
  "stem": "Given that de Moivre's theorem holds for positive integers, prove that it also holds when $n = -m$, where $m$ is a positive integer.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 writes $(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^{-m} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos m\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin m\\theta}$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 multiplies by the conjugate: denominator $\\cos^2 m\\theta + \\sin^2 m\\theta = 1$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 obtains $\\cos m\\theta - \\mathrm{i}\\sin m\\theta = \\cos(-m\\theta) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin(-m\\theta) = \\cos n\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin n\\theta$.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\n(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^{-m} = \\frac{1}{(\\cos\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^m} = \\frac{1}{\\cos m\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin m\\theta}\n$$\n$$\n= \\frac{\\cos m\\theta - \\mathrm{i}\\sin m\\theta}{\\cos^2 m\\theta + \\sin^2 m\\theta} = \\cos m\\theta - \\mathrm{i}\\sin m\\theta\n$$\n$$\n= \\cos(-m\\theta) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin(-m\\theta) = \\cos n\\theta + \\mathrm{i}\\sin n\\theta\n$$"
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "Use the positive-integer result, then the conjugate, then cos(−x) = cos x and sin(−x) = −sin x.",
  "hints": [
   "Write the negative power as a reciprocal."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "calculator": false
 },
